# The PC/SC daemon, without which a smartcard is invisible to gpg.
#
# WHY IT IS A SERVICE AND NOT SOMETHING gpg STARTS. scdaemon does not spawn
# pcscd and cannot: it dlopens libpcsclite.so.1 and that library connects to a
# socket somebody else is already listening on. So the package can be built,
# installed and correct, and every smartcard operation still fails with no
# reader found -- which is the same shape as the seatd bug, where the binary
# was installed and nothing started it, and the error named DRM rather than
# the daemon that was missing.
#
# type = process BECAUSE IT MUST KEEP RUNNING, and --foreground is what makes
# that true. pcscd daemonises by default; under dinit that would fork, exit,
# and be treated as a service that terminated immediately. -f keeps it in the
# foreground where dinit can supervise it.
#
# NO ready-notification, AND UNLIKE seatd THAT IS FINE. seatd needed one
# because labwc started the instant dinit saw the fork and then found no
# socket. Nothing here starts at boot that needs pcscd: it is consulted the
# first time a person runs gpg against a card, which is minutes into a
# session rather than milliseconds into a boot. There is also no fd-notify
# option to use -- that path in pcscd is systemd's sd_notify, and this build
# passes -Dlibsystemd=false.
#
# --hotplug IS NOT PASSED AND THE DEFAULT IS WHAT WE WANT. pcscd rescans the
# USB bus on its own; `pcscd --hotplug` is a one-shot command that tells an
# already-running daemon to rescan, not a mode to start it in. Passing it here
# would make this service a request to a daemon that does not exist yet.
#
# THE SOCKET DIRECTORY IS pcscd's TO MAKE. pcscdaemon.c:626 creates
# /run/pcscd with mode 0755 and chmods it afterwards so a restrictive umask
# cannot make it unreachable. That is what lets the unprivileged veron user
# talk to a daemon running as root -- the same boundary the rest of this
# system draws: privilege lives in a root-owned service file, and the desktop
# reaches it through a socket rather than by becoming root.
#
# depends-on early-filesystems BECAUSE /run MUST BE A tmpfs FIRST. The image
# root is read-only and pcscd creates its directory under /run.
#
# depends-on device-nodes BECAUSE libusb NEEDS /dev/bus/usb. This build of
# libusb has udev disabled and enumerates through sysfs, but it still opens
# the character devices under /dev/bus/usb, and those come from devtmpfs
# being mounted. Without this, pcscd starts, finds no bus to scan, and
# reports no readers on a machine that has one plugged in.
type = process
command = /usr/sbin/pcscd --foreground
restart = true
depends-on = early-filesystems
depends-on = device-nodes
